ozz-checked-allocation: report a refused allocation instead of writing through it

Upstream ozz writes through every allocation it makes without checking it.
Skeleton::Allocate, Animation::Allocate and Track::Allocate build spans over
a null block and their Load and builders fill them; ozz::New placement-news
at address zero; a suppressed log level constructs its local stream through
ozz::New; MemoryStream::Resize copies the old buffer into a null new one and
drops the old; StdAllocator hands a standard container a null block, which
it constructs into.

Animation::Allocate and Track::Allocate return false, and Skeleton::Allocate
null, when the allocation fails; each Load then leaves its object empty and
records it on the archive (IArchive::FailAllocation, out_of_memory), which
reads nothing more after it, as after a refusal (stopped). SkeletonBuilder,
AnimationBuilder and TrackBuilder return null when the object or its buffer
cannot be allocated; a skeleton with no joints allocates no buffer and still
builds. ozz::New returns null on a refused allocation. A suppressed log line
writes to a discarding stream when its local one cannot be allocated.
MemoryStream::Resize keeps its buffer and returns false. StdAllocator, which
a container cannot see fail and which runs without exceptions, aborts on a
refused allocation rather than handing it null; every count an archive
gives a container is checked first (ozz-archive-counts.patch), so only a
real exhaustion reaches it.

Evidence: zozz's tests/allocation.cpp drives each site with the allocation
refused; upstream faults in every one ("constructor call on null pointer",
"applying non-zero offset 160 to null pointer", access violations at low
addresses; measured, 2026-10-31).

